WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.950
Welcome to divisibility and remainders.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.840
A remainder is what is left after you have made as many

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.410
equal groups as you can.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.940
That tiny number can tell you when a cycle repeats or

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.550
which of several numbers can possibly be the answer.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.200
In AMC Upper Primary practice, look for clues such as

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.450
divides evenly, left over, and repeats every.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.420
In a Rossmoyne style number puzzle, several clues can

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.790
apply to the same number.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.130
We will turn each clue into a test.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.630
Start with thirty-eight stickers.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.690
Each full packet holds six.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.560
How many full packets can you make, and how many

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.450
stickers are left?

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.950
Picture filling the packets one at a time.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.780
Six packets use thirty-six stickers, leaving two.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.120
One more packet would need six stickers,

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.540
so two is the remainder.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.380
We can record the whole story in one equation.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.960
Thirty-eight equals six times six plus two.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.300
Now pick one of these four numbers.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.510
It must be divisible by four and leave remainder two

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:03.860
when divided by three.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.080
Which test would let you throw out choices fastest?

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.420
Every hundred is a multiple of four, so test the

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.310
last two digits.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.330
Twenty-four and twelve are multiples of four.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.750
Forty-two and twenty-one are out.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.540
One hundred and twenty-four is one past a

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.550
multiple of three.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.410
Two hundred and twelve is two past two hundred and ten,

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.590
so it fits both clues.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.130
Here is a harder puzzle.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.620
A blue beacon flashes at minute two and every six

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:36.610
minutes after that.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.700
A gold beacon flashes at minute five and every nine

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:41.810
minutes after that.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.980
When do they flash together strictly between minutes one

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.550
hundred and two hundred, at a multiple of five?

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.650
List a few early flashes.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.010
Blue goes at two, eight, and fourteen.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.250
Gold goes at five and fourteen.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:58.990
They first meet at fourteen.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.240
From there, both beacons return together every eighteen

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.240
minutes, the smallest positive time that is a multiple

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.650
of six and nine.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:08.450
Add eighteen repeatedly.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.990
Just below our range is eighty-six.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:15.460
Inside it we get one hundred and four, one hundred and

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.300
twenty-two, one hundred and forty, one hundred and

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.470
fifty-eight, one hundred and seventy-six,

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.250
and one hundred and ninety-four.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.230
Only one ends in zero or five.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.350
One hundred and forty is the answer.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.140
Check from the start.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.174
One hundred and forty minus two is one hundred and

00:02:32.174 --> 00:02:35.360
thirty-eight, or twenty-three cycles of six.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:37.878
One hundred and forty minus five is one hundred and

00:02:37.878 --> 00:02:40.690
thirty-five, or fifteen cycles of nine.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.760
And one hundred and forty is twenty-eight times five.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.120
When one cycle is six and the other is nine,

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.180
adding the periods gives fifteen.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.880
Use the smallest common multiple, eighteen.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.240
Also remember that divisible means remainder zero.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.730
Finally, keep your search inside the interval the

00:02:58.730 --> 00:02:59.720
question gives you.

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:03.570
On an exam, write the remainders next to their divisors

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.680
and combine the clues one at a time.

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.640
Check your answer against every original condition.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.910
For a Mawa style investigation, ask what changing one

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.600
starting minute would do to the meetings.
